<scene>
    <a ambient-light="color: 0x404040" />
    <a directional-light="direction: -0.4 -0.8 -0.45; intensity: 1" />

    <!-- distance 2.2 (down from the recipe's original 6, s3r 2026-09-01, `specs/shallot-tui.md` criterion 8): at
         the default distance the unit cube's 80x24 grid silhouette is ~5 rows tall, too small for the
         three faces to read as three faces. 2.2 puts the cube at ~14-18 rows across every orbit yaw this
         recipe reaches, still inside the 80x24 grid at every angle (near plane 0.1, cube half-extent
         ~0.7, so no clipping).

         pitch 0.55 (raised from 0.3, s3r review repair 2026-09-02): at pitch 0.3 the top face foreshortens
         to a bare edge band and only two of the three visible faces carry legible fill; 0.5 opens the top
         face to a real but still 2-row sliver, and 0.55 is where it clears three rows of its own fill
         glyphs while the cube still stays inside the 80x24 grid
         (`packages/shallot/scripts/dump-cells-ascii.ts`'s own dump confirms both at this orbit). -->
    <a id="camera" camera sear transform orbit="distance: 2.2; yaw: 0.6; pitch: 0.55" />

    <!-- lit, view-independent by construction (corrected 2026-09-01, `specs/shallot-tui.md`'s locked
         glyph-selection addendum): the box never rotates (only the camera orbits it) and the light is
         fixed in world space, so each face's normal·light angle — and so its luminance and its slice
         of the glyph ramp — stays constant across every camera angle. A single constant albedo with no
         lighting term at all (the prior `surface: unlit`) gives every visible face the *same* constant
         value, so the face boundaries vanish and the cube reads as one silhouette blob — exactly the
         defect this criterion exists to catch. Per-face-normal Lambertian shading against a fixed light
         keeps view-independence while still separating the faces, because a face's normal (not the
         camera) is what its luminance tracks. -->
    <a id="box" part transform color="rgba: 0.85 0.55 0.35" />
</scene>
